cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Denoising under a data-processing ceiling: observer-dependent benefits, fidelity–task divergence, and an information floor"
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Yamamoto
    given-names: Shuji
    email: yamamoto@lisit.jp
    affiliation: "Institute of One, LISIT Co., Ltd., Tokyo, Japan"
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9211-1071"
repository-code: "https://github.com/Institute-of-One/denoiq-core"
abstract: >-
  A simulation study of when image denoising helps and when it hurts a detection task, under a
  fixed data-processing ceiling. On synthetic signal-known-exactly phantoms spanning dose, noise
  correlation length and lesion configuration, three deterministic denoisers are evaluated
  against the analytic ideal-observer detectability of their own input with a leakage-controlled
  cross-fitted prewhitening observer, over ten independent realisations. It quantifies how often
  fidelity gains coincide with task losses, how the effect of denoising depends on observer
  efficiency, and how failure patterns distribute relative to an operational information floor -
  the contour at which the input's ideal-observer detectability falls below a prespecified task
  requirement, which is a task requirement rather than a zero-information boundary. Everything is
  synthetic, analytic and seeded; there is no patient data, no scanner calibration and no
  absolute exposure threshold. Model observers come from taskiq-core.
keywords:
  - denoising
  - task-based image quality
  - model observer
  - detectability
  - data processing inequality
  - fidelity-task divergence
  - observer efficiency
  - operational detectability floor
  - medical imaging
  - reproducible research
license: MIT
version: 0.1.0
date-released: "2026-07-30"
